# KNOWN GAP — `==` on two EQUAL tuples returns `false`.
#
#     auto a = (1, 2)
#     auto b = (1, 2)
#     print(a == b)          # false   <- WRONG, `true` expected
#
# `int == int` and `String == String` are both correct, so it is not the
# element types; it is the tuple aggregate itself. The comparison answers
# ADDRESS IDENTITY rather than structure.
#
# ⚖ RATIFIED 2026-08-27 (D46): TUPLES GET INTRINSIC STRUCTURAL EQUALITY
# whenever their elements are comparable. A tuple is an anonymous product type
# -- there is nowhere to write `@derive(Equatable)` on one -- so requiring a
# derive is impossible and rejecting would make tuples second-class.
#
# ⚠ This is DISTINCT from the missing-`Equatable`-impl defect on structs and
# enums, which D46 rules must be a check-time REJECTION. Tuples must WORK;
# structs and enums must be REFUSED until the author derives. Same ruling,
# opposite outcomes, because a tuple has no place to put the derive.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints true.
# Measured 2026-08-27; orchestrator-verified.

void main():
    auto a = (1, 2)
    auto b = (1, 2)
    print(a == b)
